<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">A Christmas Carol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as A Christmas Carol. In Prose. Being a Ghost Story of Christmas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Carol is a novella by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a>, first published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by Chapman &amp; Hall in 1843 with illustrations by John Leech. It tells of Ebenezer Scrooge, an elderly miser who is visited by the ghost of his former business partner Jacob Marley and the spirits of Christmas Past, Present and Yet to Come, and becomes a kinder man. Dickens was inspired by a visit to the Field Lane Ragged School for London's street children. Published on 19 December, the first edition sold out by Christmas Eve, and thirteen editions had appeared by the end of 1844. The book has never been out of print, and Dickens gave public readings of it from 1849 until 1870, the year of his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/a-christmas-carol.jpg" alt="A Christmas Carol" width="330" height="273" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Leech · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Dickens-A_Christmas_Carol-Title_page-First_edition_1843.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>By the end of 1842 Dickens was a well-established author, but his serial Martin Chuzzlewit sold disappointingly, and by mid-1843 his wife Catherine was pregnant with their fifth child. In early 1843 he toured the Cornish tin mines and was angered by the sight of children at work, and the Second Report of the Children's Employment Commission, published in February, horrified him. He began the book in October 1843, and his biographer Michael Slater describes it as written at white heat, completed in six weeks. Dickens built much of it in his head on night walks of 15 to 20 miles around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He drew on Washington Irving's essays on old English Christmas traditions and on essays by Douglas Jerrold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The novella is divided into five chapters that Dickens called staves. On Christmas Eve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, seven years after the death of his partner Jacob Marley, the miser Ebenezer Scrooge refuses his nephew Fred's dinner invitation. Marley's ghost warns him that three spirits will visit. The Ghost of Christmas Past shows him his lonely boyhood and the end of his engagement to Belle. The Ghost of Christmas Present shows the Bob Cratchit family's feast, the seriously ill Tiny Tim, and two emaciated children named Ignorance and Want. The Ghost of Christmas Yet to Come shows Tiny Tim's death and a neglected grave bearing Scrooge's name. Scrooge wakes changed and raises his clerk's pay. Academics discuss whether the story is fully secular or a Christian allegory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book was published on 19 December 1843 at five shillings, and the first run of 6,000 copies sold out by Christmas Eve. Reviews were almost uniformly kind. In January 1844 Parley's Illuminated Library issued an unauthorised condensed version; Dickens sued and won, but the publishers declared themselves bankrupt and he was left to pay £700 in costs. He gave public readings of an abbreviated version 127 times until 1870, the year of his death. The phrase Merry Christmas, known since 1534, was popularised by the book, and the name Scrooge entered the Oxford English Dictionary as a word for a miser in 1982.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “A Christmas Carol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>A Christmas Carol</b>, by Charles Dickens, was published in London on 19 December 1843.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles Dickens published <b>A Christmas Carol</b> on 19 December 1843; the first edition sold out by Christmas Eve, and the book has never been out of print.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles Dickens refers to it in <b>A Christmas Carol</b> (1843), changing &quot;rest&quot; to &quot;bless&quot;, and George Eliot quotes it in Silas Marner (1861).</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-rest-ye-merry-gentlemen">God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen</a></li></ul></section>
